  "instagram_caption": "Late e-invoice? The invoice portal will reject it.\n\nSince April 1, 2025, GST taxpayers with annual aggregate turnover of ₹10 crore or more get 30 days from the invoice date to report an e-invoice on the IRP. After that, the portal does not accept it. This covers invoices, credit notes and debit notes, and your buyer may struggle to claim input tax credit.\n\nCheck pending invoices every week, not at month end, and talk to your CA about your process.\n\nTry a live demo with sample data at resolveai.cc, no sign-up.\n\nSource: GSTN advisory as reported by Business Today; TallyPrime 2026 e-invoicing guide.\n\nHow often does your team check for invoices not yet reported?\n\n#resolveai #gst #einvoice #distributor #msme",
  "youtube_title": "GST E-Invoice: Report Within 30 Days or It Gets Rejected",
  "youtube_description": "If your turnover is ₹10 crore or more, you get 30 days from the invoice date to report a GST e-invoice on the IRP. After that, the portal does not accept it, and that covers credit notes and debit notes too.\n\nTry a live demo with sample data at https://www.resolveai.cc (no sign-up).\n\nSources:\n• Business Today, \"GST rule change: From April 2025, taxpayers need to upload e-invoices within 30 days of issuance\" (fetched Nov 17, 2026)\n• TallyPrime, \"What Changed In E-Invoicing Compliance In 2026?\" (fetched Nov 17, 2026)\n\nResolveAI: ERP software and AI automation for established businesses. Instagram: @resolveai.software\n\n#Shorts #ResolveAI #GST"
